   Markov Chain Monte Carlo: a type of Monte Carlo method. Say that you have $P$ up to proportionality, and call this $P*$. You choose a translation kernel Q (in the finite case, representable as a stochastic matrix from states to states) and an acceptance criterion (Metropolis Hastings is one common one) and then take a random walk. Given some assumptions, you can prove that a distribution over states induced by the random walk will eventually converge to the stationary distribution (unit eigenvector) of Q, which given some assumptions, is P. In other words, the states in your Markov chain are assignments to all the variables of the distribution (it could be a joint distribution) and so the stationary distribution of the walk is a distribution over the relevant variable. Question: What is the full form of the acronym MCMC? Answer: Markov Chain Monte Carlo
